<%# Where this draft's submission stands, read from the stored request on the
    # author's own page.
    #
    # **It is rendered from the request rather than from the draft's status**, because
    # the two come apart exactly where the author most needs an answer: a rejected
    # submission hands the draft back, so the draft says `draft` again while the
    # request is the only row that carries the reviewer's reason. A page that read
    # the status would report "still a draft" to the person whose work was just
    # refused.
    #
    # **It shows the reviewer's notes, not just the badge.** `ReviewRequest` requires
    # a rejection to say why, and those notes are written *for the author* — the
    # reviewer's own page says so on the field. Before this, they were stored and
    # only ever printed where the reviewer stood, which made the requirement a
    # message into a place the author cannot reach.
    #
    # A withdrawn submission is the fourth answer and says what it is: the author
    # took it back, nobody reviewed it, and nothing was written.
    #
    # It takes the request as a local rather than reading the draft, so this surface
    # and the controller agree about *which* submission is being described — the
    # newest one, because a rejected handover is history and the draft goes back to
    # being working work.
    %>
<div class="list-group-item draft-submission-status">
  <div class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2 flex-wrap">
    <span class="badge rounded-pill text-bg-light border draft-submission-badge">
      <%= t("review_requests.statuses.#{review_request.status}") %>
    </span>
    <span><%= t("drafts.submission_status.heading") %></span>
  </div>

  <div class="entity-description draft-submission-state">
    <%= t("drafts.submission_status.#{review_request.status}_html",
      reviewer: record_label(review_request.reviewed_by),
      at: time_tag(review_request.created_at, l(review_request.created_at, format: :short))) %>
  </div>

  <%# The author's own sentence back to them, because a handover is a statement
      about one moment and this is the moment it was made. %>
  <% if review_request.submission_message.present? %>
    <div class="entity-description draft-submission-message">
      <%= t("drafts.submission_status.message", message: review_request.submission_message) %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <% if review_request.review_notes.present? %>
    <div class="entity-description draft-submission-notes">
      <%= t("drafts.submission_status.notes", notes: review_request.review_notes) %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>